WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.080
SIERRA: Nuclear energy.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.850
Back to the Future: Are you telling me that
this sucker is nuclear?

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:21.210
No, no, no, no, no, this sucker is electrical.
I need a nuclear reaction to generate the

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.860
1.21 gigawatts of electricity I need.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.500
The Simpsons: Watch out, Radioactive Man!

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.640
Justice League Unlimited: There s a breach
in the reactor room. We are getting radiation

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:34.640
alarms from several lower floors.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.640
Young Sheldon: It s clean and efficient and
very safe, until something goes horribly wrong.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:36.640
The Simpsons: It s the job of tomorrow, today!

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.480
SIERRA: A lot of our ideas about nuclear energy
come from what we have seen on the big screen

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:49.079
or on TV. Mutants, supervillains, and time-traveling
cars, sure, but also the haunting legacies

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:57.840
of real-life disasters. Nuclear is just unsettling.
After all, no other energy source leaves behind

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:05.689
radioactive waste that lasts for tens of thousands
of years. But nuclear energy is also widely

00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:11.799
misunderstood. And as the climate crisis accelerates,
many experts believe that it may be one of

00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:17.490
the best tools we have for eliminating our
dependence on fossil fuels. I m Gabrielle

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:24.070
Sierra, and this is Why It Matters, today,
the truth about nuclear energy in the era

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:26.650
of climate change.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:34.700
Reuters: Representatives from nearly 200 countries
will convene in Glasgow, Scotland at the end

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.520
of October for the COP26 conference to strengthen
action against global warming.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.370
Greenpeace USA: No! Nukes! No! Nukes!

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:48.750
KPBS: So, what s your biggest fear? A meltdown,
a spent fuel fire, sabotage, tsunami, earthquake,

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.939
man-made error, human mistakes, engineering
mistakes, not sure which one s biggest.

00:01:51.939 --> 00:01:56.620
Forbes Breaking News: Nuclear energy, it s
a non-green house gas power generation source

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.010
and a crucial tool in the battle against climate
change.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:07.479
SIERRA: All right, well, I mean let's talk
about the big question. What role is nuclear

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:14.270
energy currently playing in the battle against
climate change? And what role do you think

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:15.980
it should play?

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:22.780
DEWAN: It's my belief that nuclear is crucial
for moving the world away from fossil fuels.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:28.630
DEWAN: I'm Leslie Dewan. I'm a nuclear engineer
and I run a radiation detection company called

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:34.300
RadiantNano. And I became a nuclear engineer
in the first place, because I've always been

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:39.980
an environmentalist. Right now, nuclear is
doing a lot of the heavy lifting. So between

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:47.260
about a half and two thirds of the carbon
free electricity in the US comes from nuclear,

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.970
somewhat similar numbers worldwide.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:54.970
SIERRA: So let s put this into perspective
a bit. In 2020, fossil fuels generated about

00:02:54.970 --> 00:03:01.630
60 percent of the electricity in the United
States. Nuclear accounted for roughly 20 percent,

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:07.340
that s about the same as solar, wind, and
all other renewables combined. Now it s important

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:13.110
to understand that electricity production
is only part of the overall carbon picture,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:19.099
but it is a big part. In 2019, electricity
production accounted for about a quarter of

00:03:19.099 --> 00:03:24.840
all global carbon emissions. Even if the world
got that down to zero, there would still be

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:31.349
emissions from cars, the shipping industry,
and many other sources. But why use nuclear

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:37.610
power at all? Why not stick to safer options
like wind, and solar?

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:44.890
DEWAN: So the big advantage of nuclear is
the base load energy supply. Basically, it

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:51.140
can produce a constant amount of power without
needing to store electricity in battery backups.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:56.540
So I'm 100% in favor of expanding the amount
of solar and wind that we're using, but the

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:02.040
tricky thing with wind especially is that
you can have days when you have a lot of wind

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.820
and there's a large oversupply of electricity,
and you need to find a way to store it. Or

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:11.760
you can have days where there isn't any wind
at all and you need to find a way to make

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:18.040
up for that shortfall. So nuclear is very,
very good at basically helping to smooth out

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.090
those peaks and troughs in supply.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:26.479
JACKSON: We are all, I think, more interested
today in having as low a carbon footprint

00:04:26.479 --> 00:04:28.970
in our activities as we can.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:34.850
SIERRA: This is Dr. Shirley Ann Jackson. She
is the president of the Rensselaer Polytechnic

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:40.960
Institute, and Institute professor of physics,
applied physics and astronomy, as well as

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.440
a professor of engineering sciences. In 1995
Dr. Jackson was appointed by President Clinton

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.370
to run the US Nuclear Regulatory commission,
meaning that she was the chief authority on

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:59.130
nuclear safety in the United States. She has
been a global leader in the effort to advance

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:02.780
and safely regulate nuclear power for decades.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:09.870
JACKSON: And obviously power generation is
important for maintaining societies and uplifting

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:16.840
lives. And so we have to work out a plan based
on all possible energy solutions, and with

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:24.180
the high energy density, it's reliability
when well-managed, and the low carbon footprint

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:30.690
of nuclear plants, I think we have to think
about nuclear energy as part of an overall

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:35.090
strategy. And that is why it should matter
to us.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:39.090
SIERRA: How safe is nuclear energy?

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:46.901
JACKSON: Well, what people worry about is
exposure. People think about radiation. They

00:05:46.901 --> 00:05:53.270
think maybe when they see vapor that really
comes from cooling towers at a nuclear plant,

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:58.500
they're thinking that it's a radioactive release,
it is not. Some fear that there might be a

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:05.069
release in wastewater coming from the plant.
And there are myriad systems that are built

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:10.780
in to keep that from happening. People are
not just willy-nilly operating things in an

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:17.729
unsafe manner. And so that, I believe, should
help to allay people's fears.

00:06:17.729 --> 00:06:24.960
The safeguard systems that Dr. Jackson referenced
do a very good job of preventing nuclear disasters

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:31.960
in the United States. That said, 49 percent
of Americans oppose nuclear energy and only

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:38.220
47 percent of Americans believe nuclear power
is safe. Part of the problem is that most

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.919
of us don t understand what s happening inside
those plants.

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:48.349
SIERRA: Can you give me a 101 on the science
of how nuclear energy works?

00:06:48.349 --> 00:06:56.099
DEWAN: Nuclear energy is basically just a
fancy way of boiling water. So within a nuclear

00:06:56.099 --> 00:07:02.020
reactor core, you have your uranium oxide
fuel rods, they're cooled and moderated by

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:08.710
liquid water. And the fuel rods are in a configuration
where they've achieved what's called criticality.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:14.460
So they have a large stable number of nuclear
fission reactions that are generating a great

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.860
deal of heat. That heat is carried away by
the water flowing past them, boils the water

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:26.400
up into steam, the steam drives the turbine
that turns the generator that produces electricity.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.580
SIERRA: Nuclear fission is the process by
which atoms split, creating enormous amounts

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:38.190
of energy. Nuclear reactors are basically
just structures that are able to control this

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:44.160
process in a safe and useful way. But not
all reactors are the same.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:50.729
SIERRA: So let's talk about reactors. What
do they do and are there different types?

00:07:50.729 --> 00:07:57.479
DEWAN: So the majority of nuclear reactors
operating today are what's called light water

00:07:57.479 --> 00:08:02.610
reactors. They re sometimes called conventional
reactors or traditional reactors. These reactors

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:08.350
are actually not first developed for power
production, not for really producing electricity,

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:14.090
they were actually first developed in the
1950s for nuclear powered submarines. So they

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:20.020
came out of the US Navy initially. And then
it wasn't until around 1956 at the height

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:24.960
of the Cold War when the US said, all right,
we want to have nuclear reactors used for

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.220
power on land. And as a point of national
pride, we want to get these up and running

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:33.770
as quickly as possible. We want to get this
done specifically before the Soviet Union

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:39.310
does. And so they took these submarine based
reactors and put them on land. And that means

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:46.300
that there's a lot of room for improvement.
And so that's what the next generation of

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:50.480
reactors, the so-called advanced reactors,
are trying to adapt to, trying to basically

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.410
make nuclear reactors that are impeccably
well suited for use on land. And there are

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:59.269
a few different varieties of these advanced
reactors. So the one closest to my heart is

00:08:59.269 --> 00:09:04.260
called a molten salt reactor that uses liquid
fuel rather than solid fuel. There are also

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:10.760
reactors that use uranium metal as fuel and
they're cooled and moderated by either liquid

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:16.910
sodium or liquid lead. Those are called metal
fast reactors. And then there are reactors

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:23.200
cooled by gas, reactors that use, instead,
fuel rods, pebbles of fuel. And they all have

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:29.110
different safety benefits and some of their
own new drawbacks as well. But overall, the

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:35.390
hope is that these new reactors will be much
more lower cost, more proliferation resistant

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:38.620
and easier to roll out and higher efficiency
as well.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:44.040
SIERRA: I love that you described a reactor
as being close to your heart.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.360
SIERRA: These new reactors are designed to
address the weak points of nuclear energy.

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:57.180
And there are weak points. The problems fall
into four basic buckets: cost, waste, security,

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:02.290
and the rare but serious possibility of disaster.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:11.100
WNEP: An unexpected release of more radiation
today from the Three Mile Island nuclear power

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:12.100
plant has led to a series of consequences.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:15.110
ABC News In-depth: Massive quantities of radiation
have apparently been released at an accident

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:17.420
at the Chernobyl power station in the Ukraine.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:24.360
TODAY: Fukushima. It began with the strongest
earthquake in Japan s history, one of the

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.660
world s worst nuclear disasters.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:35.240
DEWAN: I think that s something that the nuclear
industry needs to be really, really open about,

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:41.700
talking about what went wrong in those accidents,
talking about how the existing nuclear facilities

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:48.750
are different from that previous generation
of plants. So what went wrong in all three

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:56.329
of those cases in Three Mile Island, Chernobyl
and Fukushima is basically those plants lost

00:10:56.329 --> 00:11:02.769
their electricity and then lost the cooling
water that went over the uranium oxide fuel

00:11:02.769 --> 00:11:08.060
rods. So lost electricity to the pumps, the
pumps the cooling water, cooling water drained

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:14.440
away. And then your uranium oxide fuel rods
would heat up and heat up and heat up with

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:19.350
nothing to cool them down. And eventually
they heated up so much that the structural

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:24.769
integrity of the fuel rods was compromised.
They started to bend, they started to melt.

00:11:24.769 --> 00:11:30.829
And that's what a nuclear meltdown is. The
newer generation of nuclear reactors have

00:11:30.829 --> 00:11:36.459
very, very different cooling requirements.
And so in many cases, it's just not possible

00:11:36.459 --> 00:11:41.209
for them to have a meltdown, like in the case
of a molten salt reactor. And so I think that's

00:11:41.209 --> 00:11:46.440
an important piece to get across is that the
technology of these advanced reactors is so

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:53.089
different that it really obviates some of
the prior failure modes that were there.

00:11:53.089 --> 00:11:57.481
SIERRA: Meltdowns are terrifying and tragic,
but it would be a big mistake to think that

00:11:57.481 --> 00:12:02.560
nuclear is the most deadly power source. A
recent Harvard study found that air pollution

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:11.080
from fossil fuel combustion was responsible
for 8.7 million deaths globally in 2018 alone.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:20.600
By comparison, nuclear power has caused about
5,000 deaths in a 38 year period from 1971-2009.

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:25.550
Deaths from nuclear power are typically caused
by onsite accidents, whereas deaths from fossil

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:30.920
fuels are largely caused by the air pollution
they create. Exact comparisons are difficult,

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:33.329
but the difference in scale is obvious.

00:12:33.329 --> 00:12:40.279
JACKSON: Well, I actually visited Chernobyl
in my first year as chairman of the NRC, and

00:12:40.279 --> 00:12:47.600
that does focus the mind. And I actually visited
the site, and I had to wear every dosimeter

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:54.230
known to man or woman. Both ones that would
measure longer term exposure over the time

00:12:54.230 --> 00:13:00.530
I was there, as well as what are called alarming
dosimeters if things start to get hot on the

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:05.800
spot. And then I had to go to NIH before I
left for a whole body scan, and another one

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:11.779
when I got back. But I felt it was important
to understand what countries were dealing

00:13:11.779 --> 00:13:22.240
with, in this case Ukraine. Any technology
sits on a knife edge, and there can be sometimes

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:30.350
accidents or sometimes things may be deliberately
used for ill. But on average, nuclear is much

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:38.060
more regulated, and has much more oversight
than your typical fossil generating station

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:45.530
or your typical chemical plant that people
deal with every day. And so I would not, as

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:50.769
a former chairman of the Nuclear Regulatory
Commission, tell you that one can just operate

00:13:50.769 --> 00:13:57.269
these facilities willy-nilly. But at the same
time, I would say you have to ask yourself

00:13:57.269 --> 00:14:04.050
as you look over a time, how much has happened
because of a nuclear accident compared to

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:07.600
chemical plant accidents and the like.

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:12.529
SIERRA: So the idea sounds like if you do
it safely, and the right precautions are put

00:14:12.529 --> 00:14:17.210
in place, the right oversight, there shouldn't
be this impulse of not in my backyard. It

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:21.740
should be a pretty safe thing to have around
a town.

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:28.029
JACKSON: I would say so, but citizens have
the right to demand that there be that appropriate

00:14:28.029 --> 00:14:34.070
oversight, and that there be transparency
about that oversight. And I always, as chair,

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:39.420
felt that that was my responsibility, and
talking with people. So whenever I visited

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:45.620
a nuclear power plant, I also met with the
local population as well as having a press

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:51.020
conference even if there was nothing in particular
to talk about, but it was important to answer

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:53.310
people's questions.

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:59.171
SIERRA: Disasters are rare, but there s another
problem that will always be part of the nuclear

00:14:59.171 --> 00:15:06.880
equation. Radioactive waste. Nuclear reactors
use radioactive material like uranium for

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:12.250
fuel. The problem is that once the process
is finished, there s still a lot of radioactive

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:18.940
stuff leftover, stuff that will remain deadly
for tens of thousands of years. So the question

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:24.490
becomes one of figuring out how to store this
material as safely as possible. In the United

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:29.889
States, spent fuel from nuclear power plants
is put into rods and stored in cooling pools

00:15:29.889 --> 00:15:35.500
at or near the site itself. But these sites
are considered temporary. Scientists agree

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:41.940
that the most promising long-term solution
is to store radioactive materials deep underground,

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:47.310
and there have been plans to develop such
a facility at Yucca Mountain, Nevada for decades.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:51.389
But so far it has not been politically feasible.

00:15:51.389 --> 00:15:57.920
JACKSON: There are really two kinds of nuclear
waste that people think about the most. One

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:04.290
is what's called low level waste. And that's
waste that where the radioactivity is low,

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:11.780
and where the disposal is easier, and can
be disposed of in fairly shallow types of

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:18.079
repositories. The problem comes with high
level waste, and spent fuel is the type that

00:16:18.079 --> 00:16:23.880
one thinks about. And the fuel comes in the
form of rods. And these spent fuel rods are

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:31.110
very highly radioactive. They have very long
live radioactivity and they generate a lot

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:38.370
of heat. And so they require very careful
handling over a very long period. And so we

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:46.120
actually have about 70,000 metric tons of
spent fuel around the country. And about 2000

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:53.810
metric tons are generated every year. And
by 2055, the belief is we'll have about twice

00:16:53.810 --> 00:17:02.060
as much. And this is spread around at 75 different
operating and decommissioned nuclear plants

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:11.120
all across the United States. And worldwide,
I think the estimate is about 300,000 a metric

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:17.740
tons. But to this point, all efforts to create
a permanent deep geologic repository have

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:19.010
failed.

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:24.160
DEWAN: There are some nuclear reactors today,
like some of the ones that are operating in

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:27.959
France that do what's called reprocessing
spent nuclear fuel. So you take the spent

00:17:27.959 --> 00:17:33.710
fuel that's coming out of a reactor, deconstruct
it, repartition it, put it into new nuclear

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:39.059
fuel assemblies and you're able to extract
some additional energy from it, say, another

00:17:39.059 --> 00:17:43.240
three or four or five percent of the energy
that you could potentially get out of the

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:49.179
uranium. There are some other companies and
research groups today that are working on

00:17:49.179 --> 00:17:54.500
making that much better, being able to extract
even more of the energy out of the spent nuclear

00:17:54.500 --> 00:18:00.370
fuel, so you're leaving behind less waste
in turn. But that problem is definitely not

00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:05.309
solved yet. And even with those reprocessing
facilities, there's still waste coming out

00:18:05.309 --> 00:18:11.380
of the backend of the design. And so people
are figuring out better ways to store that

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:17.250
in the long term, because this is waste that
you need to keep a handle on for on the order

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:22.010
of 10,000 years. So it's very much a geological
timescale problem.

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:28.080
SIERRA: Dealing with radioactive waste is
tricky and dangerous. It s also expensive.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:32.909
And advanced safety protocols are only part
of the reason that nuclear power plants are

00:18:32.909 --> 00:18:37.710
a costly investment. There s also all the
materials and technology that go into building

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:42.490
the facility, and then the operational and
maintenance costs required to keep it running

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:45.370
under a strict regulatory system.

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:52.190
DEWAN: The expensive nuclear is, to my mind,
one of the biggest aspects that's holding

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:57.520
back the construction of more nuclear power
plants. Some of the reactors that are being

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:04.130
built today in the US and Western Europe have
very, very significant cost overruns. And

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:08.169
these are plants that would have been, you
know, their initial estimates were still very

00:19:08.169 --> 00:19:13.210
expensive. You have a plant that was initially
expected to cost $7 or $8 billion, it ends

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:20.429
up costing $13 billion. And that's just not
sustainable. And so I think this isn't the

00:19:20.429 --> 00:19:25.340
most glamorous part of engineering, but I
think there's so much potential for just streamlining

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:31.260
the construction process, making more modular
nuclear reactors. There's a whole category

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:36.539
called small modular nuclear reactors where
in general, you'd be producing all of the

00:19:36.539 --> 00:19:41.770
components at a central location and then
shipping out the reactors to where they're

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:47.830
ultimately going to be installed. And I think
that decreasing that overall cost, increasing

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:52.940
modularity while still keeping the same safety
benefits, I think will be crucial to building

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:54.900
more nuclear worldwide.

00:19:54.900 --> 00:20:00.340
SIERRA: The last issue with nuclear power
doesn t have to do with the technology itself,

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:06.270
but with the possibility that it could be
attacked, stolen, or exploited by adversaries.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:15.370
SIERRA: What type of terrorism risks do nuclear
power plants present? Can someone hack them

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:16.940
and cause a disaster?

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:23.429
JACKSON: Well, what ends up happening with
most nuclear plants frankly, is that the nuclear

00:20:23.429 --> 00:20:30.059
plants don't tend to be directly connected
to the internet. And so that's not to say

00:20:30.059 --> 00:20:36.220
that one, people don't think about how they
might reach into a plant because nefarious

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:44.130
actors will always do that, but the plants
are designed to not be so easily accessible,

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:51.940
and it's not something that is discussed publicly.
Secondly, the plants do have controls which

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:59.669
tend to shut them down very quickly if, in
fact, there is some irregularity. And so that's

00:20:59.669 --> 00:21:01.011
all I would really say about that.

00:21:01.011 --> 00:21:07.740
SIERRA: But a direct attack on a nuclear plant,
whether cyber or traditional, is not the only

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:09.200
security fear.

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:16.130
DEWAN: So what you need to be really careful
about is monitoring the nuclear material that's

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:21.280
going into the plant in the form of fuel rods
or any other type of fuel form rather, and

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:27.169
then also tracking the material that's leaving
the plant. So when you take out your spent

00:21:27.169 --> 00:21:32.230
fuel rods from the reactor and put them in
a spent fuel pool to cool them down or put

00:21:32.230 --> 00:21:37.550
them into dry cask storage on the site after
you've extracted the energy that you can extract

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:42.990
from the fuel. So it wouldn't practically
speaking be possible to take that spent fuel

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:48.640
or that fresh fuel and turn it into a full
fledged nuclear bomb, but it is radioactive

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:52.590
material. And so you need to make sure that
people aren't diverting that and turning that

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:58.510
into a dirty bomb, for example. So you do
have to have very, very strict security and

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:03.710
safeguards protocols to monitor where that
is at any one time.

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:12.179
SIERRA: So let's talk a bit about the connection
between nuclear energy and nuclear weapons.

00:22:12.179 --> 00:22:17.529
What's the connection? Is there a connection?
And where do we see a potential problem playing

00:22:17.529 --> 00:22:18.529
out?

00:22:18.529 --> 00:22:25.029
DEWAN: Looking at it from an engineering perspective,
so the nuclear material that goes into weapons

00:22:25.029 --> 00:22:32.669
is very different from the nuclear material
that's used in power facilities. And if you

00:22:32.669 --> 00:22:38.330
are making a nuclear weapon that nuclear material
has to have greater than 90% or typically

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:45.909
greater than 95% uranium-235 for it to be
feasible to be used in a bomb. In nuclear

00:22:45.909 --> 00:22:54.300
power facilities, it's approximately 5% uranium-235.
So you couldn't take the uranium from a nuclear

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:58.750
reactor and turn that into a nuclear bomb.
It just would not be physically possible.

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:05.360
JACKSON: In order to get to that, it requires
additional capability in terms of the ability

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:11.840
to actually do that enrichment. And that takes
a lot of infrastructure, money, focus, and

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:19.419
time. But of course, then what gets monitored
by the organizations like the IAEA is just

00:23:19.419 --> 00:23:25.470
that whether they are facilities, be they,
most of the time, clandestine that a country

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:33.549
may have where it's looking to create weapons-grade,
uranium, or plutonium. And so those are the

00:23:33.549 --> 00:23:38.190
things that people spend a lot of time focusing
on being able to detect.

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:45.419
SIERRA: Sure. So the concern is that a nuclear
power program could be a cover for a nuclear

00:23:45.419 --> 00:23:46.419
weapons program?

00:23:46.419 --> 00:23:52.240
JACKSON: Well, that is something that, of
course, a nation would worry about, that a

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:58.000
country like ours would worry about. That's
why people worry about centrifuges in certain

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:03.740
countries, and how many stages to the centrifuges
there are because those things link to how

00:24:03.740 --> 00:24:08.770
much uranium can be enriched, and enriched
to the point that it becomes what's called

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:13.390
weapons-grade. I mean, these are very sensitive
issues as you can imagine.

00:24:13.390 --> 00:24:19.430
SIERRA: This concern isn t hypothetical. Take
Iran for example. Their nuclear energy program

00:24:19.430 --> 00:24:25.381
began in 1957, with the help of President
Eisenhower s Atoms for Peace program. Many

00:24:25.381 --> 00:24:30.470
believe this initiative set the foundation
for Iran to secretly develop a nuclear weapons

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:36.640
program by providing dual-use technology,
equipment, and education. It s just one example

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:43.770
of how nuclear technology is never a trivial
matter. And why it requires such strict regulation.

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:51.710
DEWAN: So the majority of nuclear plants in
the US are run by private energy utilities.

00:24:51.710 --> 00:24:58.150
For example, Southern Company owns and operates
the vast majority of plants within the southeastern

00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:04.630
United States. But throughout it, there's
a very, very strong government oversight process,

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:11.080
throughout basically every stage of the plant
from the early design to pouring the concrete

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:17.779
to operating the facility. And that oversight
is almost entirely done by the U.S. Nuclear

00:25:17.779 --> 00:25:21.260
Regulatory Commission at basically all stages.

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:26.520
SIERRA: Is there oversight of nuclear power
at the global level?

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:33.360
JACKSON: Oh, sure. Well, how does that occur?
I mean, it emanates from, in our case, from

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:40.170
the national to the global. And let me explain
that. There's a layered effect. So there are

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:46.470
laws in this country that govern that regulatory
oversight. They began with the Atomic Energy

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:53.240
Act which has been in existence for 70, 80
years, but it's also governed by the various

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:59.390
environmental acts, the NRC policy acts, the
Nuclear Waste Policy Act, and what's called

00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:08.270
The Energy Reorganization Act of 1974 that
created the NRC. But then that plays against

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:15.510
a larger background that links to bilateral
agreements between countries as well as work

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:22.399
through multi national organizations. And
what are they? The one that most people hear

00:26:22.399 --> 00:26:27.810
about is the IAEA, the International Atomic
Energy Agency.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:33.289
SIERRA: This organization was created to promote
the peaceful use of nuclear energy, and to

00:26:33.289 --> 00:26:39.039
prevent it from being used for military purposes.
It s a forum for cooperation on peaceful nuclear

00:26:39.039 --> 00:26:44.760
technology, but it s also a watchdog that
has the authority to conduct inspections of

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:49.130
nuclear facilities to ensure that they are
safe, and that they are not being used to

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:55.789
develop nuclear weapons. The IAEA derives
its authority from the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation

00:26:55.789 --> 00:27:01.789
of Nuclear Weapons, an agreement between 191
countries, that aims to prevent the spread

00:27:01.789 --> 00:27:03.790
of nuclear weapons.

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:09.760
JACKSON: So there, any number of international
agreements that provide an overall safety

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:16.230
envelope. In addition, there are international
agreements and treaties that relate to nuclear

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:23.860
weapons as well. And all of those are meant
to provide a framework that ensures that there's

00:27:23.860 --> 00:27:31.220
the highest level of focus and adherence to
nuclear safety, and what I call nuclear safeguards.

00:27:31.220 --> 00:27:37.210
And so these are things that play what happens
at the national level into what happens at

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:38.750
the international level.

00:27:38.750 --> 00:27:44.510
SIERRA: Does the need for regulation and safety
increase the end cost of nuclear energy?

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:49.510
DEWAN: It does increase the end cost, but
it's crucially necessary. Like, I think that

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:55.460
if there's any field at all whatsoever that
should be regulatory heavy, nuclear is certainly

00:27:55.460 --> 00:28:03.020
it. So I view all of those regulations as
being just an integral part of the process.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:07.510
And along the lines of regulation, I think
that the US Nuclear Regulatory Commission

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:14.970
has become very, very forward looking when
it comes to thinking about, say, how you regulate

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:20.169
the next generation of nuclear reactors. And
so it's been really, really interesting to

00:28:20.169 --> 00:28:24.340
me and it's a very, very welcome change that
they're starting to embrace, like what do

00:28:24.340 --> 00:28:26.260
these new designs look like.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:31.420
SIERRA: As some scientists work to refine
traditional nuclear technology, others are

00:28:31.420 --> 00:28:36.340
working on a moonshot breakthrough that could
change everything we know about energy. It

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:41.780
s called nuclear fusion, and it s the same
process that has powered the sun for billions

00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:47.580
of years. If we can replicate this reaction
on earth, it would generate virtually an unlimited

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:53.830
supply of energy, with no radioactive waste
and no greenhouse gas emissions. Scientists

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:59.610
around the world believe fusion is the energy
for the future. But safe and limitless energy

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:00.610
is no easy feat.

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:08.770
DEWAN: The 
joke is that fusion is 30 years away and always

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:14.010
will be. But if it works and there actually
has been a lot of really interesting progress

00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:18.909
on that front, just over the past few years,
it would be able to provide large amounts

00:29:18.909 --> 00:29:24.620
of electricity and much more cleanly than
nuclear fission. The tricky thing is there

00:29:24.620 --> 00:29:29.360
are a lot of scientific unknowns and a lot
of engineering unknowns. And then after you

00:29:29.360 --> 00:29:34.240
lock those down, you still need to deal with
how you construct these plants in an economical

00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:38.860
fashion. So there's a lot of work still to
be done, but there are some really brilliant

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:43.710
people and even some just fascinating private
companies that are making really great strides

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:48.500
on developing commercially viable fusion plants
for the future.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:53.580
SIERRA: Fusion would represent an unparalleled
breakthrough in the fight against climate

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:58.470
change. But it may still be a long ways off.
For now, countries have to weigh mounting

00:29:58.470 --> 00:30:04.919
climate and energy pressures against the expense
and stigma of existing nuclear technology.

00:30:04.919 --> 00:30:10.210
Some countries, like Australia for example,
have no nuclear power at all. At the other

00:30:10.210 --> 00:30:15.360
end of the spectrum you have countries like
the UK, which plans to center nuclear to achieve

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:23.350
a fossil-fuel free energy grid by 2035. China
is planning a 40% increase in nuclear capacity.

00:30:23.350 --> 00:30:29.100
India has 7 new plants under construction.
And France is setting aside 1 billion Euros

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:31.360
for small modular reactors.

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:36.409
DEWAN: So France in particular has a very
high fraction of their electricity coming

00:30:36.409 --> 00:30:41.110
from nuclear. I think right now it's about
80%. In the past it was slightly higher. Yeah.

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:46.980
And so France among every industrial country
has by far the lowest greenhouse gas production,

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:51.370
because so much of their energy comes from
nuclear. And France in particular when they

00:30:51.370 --> 00:30:56.299
were building a lot of these reactors in the
1970s were especially good about streamlining

00:30:56.299 --> 00:31:02.730
the construction process as well. They built
very, very similar reactor units across the

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:07.899
country, which allowed them to cookie cutter
style, just roll them out one after another,

00:31:07.899 --> 00:31:15.149
after another. And just streamlining the actual
construction is one of the most crucial pieces

00:31:15.149 --> 00:31:19.380
for making sure that these plants can be built
on time and on budget.

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:24.830
SIERRA: So where is the United States in all
of this? We still derive about 20 percent

00:31:24.830 --> 00:31:30.500
of our electricity from nuclear power, but
the infrastructure is aging. 12 reactors were

00:31:30.500 --> 00:31:37.029
permanently closed between 2013 and 2021,
and in many cases this led to an increased

00:31:37.029 --> 00:31:43.100
use of fossil fuels. If these trends continue,
the nuclear share of U.S. electricity generation

00:31:43.100 --> 00:31:47.559
could fall to just 11 percent by 2050.

00:31:47.559 --> 00:31:54.920
SIERRA: What is the risk if we don't pursue
further research and implementation of nuclear

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:55.920
energy?

00:31:55.920 --> 00:31:59.960
DEWAN: I think that nuclear is crucial for
hitting our climate goals. I think there's

00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:06.260
no way that we'll be able to hit our climate
goals with solar and wind and hydro and geothermal

00:32:06.260 --> 00:32:12.360
alone. To get a little bit more specific on
that, there aren't very many more places in

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:18.191
the world where we can install additional
hydro or geothermal and wind and solar. They're

00:32:18.191 --> 00:32:24.440
fantastic technologies, but we need to couple
them with better grid scale energy storage

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:29.029
or else they won't be useful for the grid.
One of the craziest things to me about the

00:32:29.029 --> 00:32:35.000
electric grid in general as a system is that
you need to across an electric grid match

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:39.410
very, very closely the amount of electricity
being produced at any one time and the amount

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:44.769
of electricity being consumed at any one time.
And you can get halfway decent estimates for

00:32:44.769 --> 00:32:48.960
how much electricity is being consumed at
any one time. You can say, okay, well this

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:52.919
is baseline, how much people in businesses
use. And okay, there's a heat wave coming

00:32:52.919 --> 00:32:56.169
up next week. So we can guess that people
are going to turn on their air conditioning

00:32:56.169 --> 00:33:03.590
units and it'll increase a little bit. But
if you couple that with an unpredictable supply

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:08.200
from solar and wind, it becomes a very, very,
very tricky problem. And again, to emphasize

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:12.740
that I'm 100% in favor of expanding solar
and wind, we definitely need to expand that

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:17.660
as well, but we also need to figure out better
types of storage in order to be able to use

00:33:17.660 --> 00:33:18.660
them properly.

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:24.580
SIERRA: And we re running out of time. Scientists
at the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:30.509
Change warn that the earth s average temperature
could hit the dangerous threshold of 1.5 degrees

00:33:30.509 --> 00:33:32.850
warming by the early 2030s.

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:38.480
SIERRA: It's fascinating to me that we need
to expand nuclear, because it felt like when

00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:43.639
I was growing up, nuclear energy was thought
of as something that was very bad for the

00:33:43.639 --> 00:33:48.409
environment. And now it seems that the reverse
is true. There's still a little time left

00:33:48.409 --> 00:33:55.799
just 10 years, according to some studies.
And this is a tool that we have that we know

00:33:55.799 --> 00:33:56.799
works.

00:33:56.799 --> 00:34:01.299
DEWAN: I think that's exactly right. I think
one of the more urgent pieces actually is

00:34:01.299 --> 00:34:06.500
when it comes down to whether you want to
keep existing nuclear power plants open or

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:12.320
not, because there's a large number of about
99 operating in the US, about 440 worldwide,

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:18.980
of this older generation of conventional light
water reactors. And there've been a number

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:24.000
of places in the US where these plants have
been shut down, say, in New England where

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:29.210
I'm originally from. Then after the old nuclear
plants are shut down, the balance of electricity

00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:35.500
production is made up for with coal power,
which is just catastrophic environmentally.

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:42.419
So I think it's really important also to not
shut down the existing facilities prematurely.

00:34:42.419 --> 00:34:48.190
They're operating safely, they've been operating
safely for 30, 40, 50 years. And you want

00:34:48.190 --> 00:34:54.850
to make sure that you keep reaping the benefit
of them. I think there is the potential for

00:34:54.850 --> 00:35:00.660
people to start having warm feelings towards
the next generation of nuclear power plants.

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:05.589
And I think I'm not completely insane in saying
that, but they can say, "Okay, that is a carbon

00:35:05.589 --> 00:35:11.190
free source of electricity. It's safe. It
is good for my community. I am glad that it's

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:19.900
part of the community." And that's my hope,
at least.

